        "headingUrdu": "حصہ 2",
        "text": "O\n\nNOT FOR SALE.\n\nThe dancing pair that simply sought renown, By holding out to tire each other down; The swain mistrustless of his smutted face, While secret laughter titter'd round the place; The bashful virgin's side-long looks of love,\n\nThe matron's glance that would those looks reprove; These were thy charms, sweet village; sports like these, With sweet succession, taught even toil to please; These round thy bowers their chearful influence shed, These were thy charms-But all these charms are fled.\n\nIll fares the land, to hast'ning ills a prey, Where wealth accumulates and men decay; Princes and lords may flourish, or may fade; A breath can make them as a breath has made; But a bold peasantry, their country's pride, When once destroy'd can never be supplied.\n\nAbout the post\n\nOliver Goldsmith was born on November 10, 1730 in Kilkenny West, Ireland and died on April 4, 1774 in London. He is best known for his novel The Vicar of Wakefield, his poem The Deserted Village (1770) and his plays The Good Natur'd Man and\n\nShe Stoops to Conquer etc. He received his BA degree in February 1749 from Trinity College, Dublin. He was the son of an Anglo- Irish Clergyman, Charles Goldsmith.\n\nGlossary\n\nWord\n\na breath\n\nbashful (adj.)\n\na word\n\nshy; timid\n\nMeaning\n\nbless (v)\n\nbowers (n)\n\ngive prayers to\n\nshaddy place surrounded by trees\n\nNOT FOR SALE.\n\nbrook (n) contending (v)\n\ncot(n)\n\ndelay'd (adj.)\n\nfor taking age\n\nfrolicked (v)\n\ngambol (v)\n\na natural stream of water\n\ncompeting with one another\n\nCottage\n\nwent on blooming late in the year\n\nfor old people, who are fond of talking\n\nwent on merrily\n\nplayful activity\n\nholding out (ph.v)\n\ni'll fares\n\nled up (ph.v.)\n\nloiter(v)\n\nmatron (n)\n\nmirthful band",
